<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umm Kulthum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Fatima Ibrahim es-Sayyid el-Beltagi, Kawkab el-Sharq, Oum Kulthum, Umm Kalthum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umm Kulthum, born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> Ibrahim es-Sayyid el-Beltagi in the village of Tamay al-Zahayra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Dakahlia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, was an Egyptian singer and film actress known by the honorific Kawkab el-Sharq, Planet of the East. The daughter of a rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, she sang religious hymns in her father's ensemble as a child, dressed as a boy, and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in the early 1920s, working with the poet Ahmed Rami and composers such as Mohammed Abdel Wahab. From 1934 she gave a broadcast concert on the first Thursday of every month for forty years, and after the 1967 war she sang abroad to raise funds for the Egyptian war effort. Her funeral in Cairo in 1975 drew a crowd of over 4 million people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umm-kulthum.jpg" alt="Umm Kulthum" width="316" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Production company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umm_Kulthum_as_Fatimah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>143 adopted Kamal El-Tawil's melody for &quot;Wallah Zaman Ya Selahy&quot;, a song with words by Salah Jahin for <b>Umm Kulthum</b> that had become popular in 1956 during the Tripartite Aggression; it was played without words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li><li><blockquote>…Silahi&quot; was first a nationalist song, written by Salah Jahin, set by Kamal Al Taweel and sung by <b>Umm Kulthum</b> during the Suez Crisis of 1956, known in Egypt and the Arab world as the Tripartite Aggression, when Egypt was invaded by the United Kingdom, France and Israel.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wallah-zaman-ya-silahi">Wallah Zaman, Ya Silahi</a></li></ul></section>
